      en: "Kuala Belalong Field Centre, established in 1991 in Ulu Temburong National Park, is a premier research facility for tropical rainforest studies. It sits at 60 m elevation and hosts scientists investigating canopy ecology, pollination, and forest regeneration. The centre's sustainable design includes rainwater harvesting and solar power. Its location within a 500-ha primary forest allows long-term monitoring of wildlife like the Bornean gibbon and the rhinoceros hornbill. Educational outreach programs train local guides and school groups in conservation practices. The centre has published over 100 peer-reviewed papers on Bornean ecology.",
